// Class 7 Science practice bank, part 3: The Ever-Evolving World of Science to Adolescence (Curiosity), more questions. Unreviewed: a teacher must check before sale.
StudyData.addPractice(7, "science", {
  "the-ever-evolving-world-of-science": {
    mcq: [
      {"q":"Which field of study is Aryabhata associated with?","options":["astronomy and mathematics","medicine and healing","surgery and operations","the scattering of light by molecules"],"answer":0,"explain":"Aryabhata worked in astronomy and mathematics. Charaka is known for medicine, Sushruta for surgery and C. V. Raman for the scattering of light.","level":1},
      {"q":"The branch of science that mainly studies substances and the changes they undergo is","options":["Physics","Chemistry","Biology","Astronomy"],"answer":1,"explain":"Chemistry studies substances and their changes. Physics deals with matter, energy and forces, and Biology with living things.","level":1},
      {"q":"Sushruta, an ancient Indian scholar, is known for his work in","options":["astronomy","the study of plant responses","surgery","the study of light"],"answer":2,"explain":"Sushruta is remembered for surgery. Aryabhata is known for astronomy, and J. C. Bose showed that plants respond to stimuli.","level":1},
      {"q":"According to the chapter, science begins with","options":["buying costly equipment","memorising old facts","winning prizes","curiosity about everyday things"],"answer":3,"explain":"Science starts with curiosity: asking 'Why?' and 'How?' about things around us, such as why leaves are green or why a ball bounces.","level":1},
      {"q":"In a fair test, how many conditions are changed at a time?","options":["Only one","Two","Every condition","Zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"A fair test changes only one thing, the variable, and keeps every other condition the same.","level":1},
      {"q":"Which of these is a safe habit in a school laboratory?","options":["Tasting a powder to find out what it is","Never tasting an unknown substance","Touching hot glass to see if it is hot","Smelling every bottle closely"],"answer":1,"explain":"Unknown substances must never be tasted, and heat, glass and chemicals should be handled carefully.","level":1},
      {"q":"C. V. Raman received the Nobel Prize in Physics in the year","options":["1900","1947","1930","1960"],"answer":2,"explain":"C. V. Raman won the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1930 for his work on the scattering of light.","level":1},
      {"q":"Which of these is a scientific attitude?","options":["Believing whatever a message says","Hiding results that look wrong","Refusing every new idea","Asking for evidence before believing a claim"],"answer":3,"explain":"A scientific attitude means being curious, honest about results, open to new ideas and asking for evidence.","level":1},
      {"q":"Sheela thinks that ants prefer sugar to salt. Which experiment can test her idea?","options":["Put equal amounts of sugar and salt at the same distance from an ant trail and count the ants at each","Put sugar close to the ant trail and the salt far away in another room","Watch the ants for one minute without giving them any food and decide","Ask her friends which of the two foods they like to eat more"],"answer":0,"explain":"A fair test gives both foods the same chance: equal amounts at equal distances. Changing the distance as well would make the result unreliable.","level":2},
      {"q":"Two bean plants are grown to find out whether light matters. Which set-up is a fair test?","options":["One plant in light with more water, one in the dark with less water","Same soil, same water and same pot size, but one plant in light and one in a dark cupboard","One plant in garden soil in light, one in sand in the dark","Both plants in light, one in a big pot and one in a small pot"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only the light should differ. In the other set-ups two things change at once, so we could not tell which one caused the result.","level":2},
      {"q":"Which of these statements is an observation rather than a hypothesis?","options":["Puddles dry faster when the weather is hotter.","Wind helps wet clothes dry quickly.","The puddle in the school ground was dry by noon.","A wider dish will dry up faster than a narrow one."],"answer":2,"explain":"An observation records something that was actually seen. The other statements are testable guesses about what causes a change, so they are hypotheses.","level":2},
      {"q":"Why are experiments repeated and the results written down carefully?","options":["So that the hypothesis is always proved right","Because writing makes the experiment shorter","So that the variable need not be controlled","So that others can check the results and trust them"],"answer":3,"explain":"Repeating and recording lets other people check the work. A result that appears again and again is more reliable.","level":2},
      {"q":"People once believed that the Sun and the planets go around the Earth. What led to the change in this belief?","options":["Careful observations that showed Earth and the other planets go around the Sun","A vote taken among the people of that time to choose an idea","A change in the weather in the places where scientists lived","The wish of the rulers of that time to have a new idea"],"answer":0,"explain":"Scientific ideas change when new evidence comes in. Careful observations supported a new idea in place of the old one.","level":2},
      {"q":"Which statement describes science, not technology?","options":["A solar cooker used in a village","Knowing that plants respond to touch and light","A weather satellite that sends cloud pictures","A mobile phone used to call a friend"],"answer":1,"explain":"Science is knowledge gained by studying nature. The other three are useful things made by applying scientific knowledge, so they are technology.","level":2},
      {"q":"To understand how a plant makes its food, a scientist needs","options":["biology alone, because a plant is a living thing","chemistry alone, because food is a substance","biology, chemistry and physics together","astronomy alone, because plants need sunlight"],"answer":2,"explain":"The branches of science overlap. Making food in a plant involves living things, substances and their changes, and energy from light.","level":2},
      {"q":"During the monsoon, the Meteorological Department wants to watch cloud movement over a large part of India. Which technology helps most?","options":["A hand lens","A kitchen weighing scale","A school ruler","A weather satellite"],"answer":3,"explain":"A satellite in space can photograph clouds over a wide area. A hand lens, a scale and a ruler cannot do this.","level":2},
      {"q":"In a class experiment, one student's result is different from those of the others. Which response shows a scientific attitude?","options":["Write the result exactly as measured and look for the reason for the difference","Copy the result of the friend sitting nearby to match the class","Change the number slightly so that it matches the others","Throw away the readings and stop doing the experiment"],"answer":0,"explain":"Scientists are honest about their results. A surprising result may lead to a better understanding, so it should be reported and investigated.","level":2},
      {"q":"Which pair of an Indian scientist and the work he is known for is correct?","options":["Sushruta – astronomy","Charaka – medicine","Aryabhata – surgery","C. V. Raman – medicine"],"answer":1,"explain":"Charaka is known for medicine. Sushruta is known for surgery, Aryabhata for astronomy and mathematics, and Raman for the scattering of light.","level":2},
      {"q":"Kavya repeats her plant experiment three times and gets very similar results each time. What does this do?","options":["It proves that her hypothesis can never be wrong","It shows that the variable was not needed","It makes her conclusion more reliable","It turns her hypothesis into a technology"],"answer":2,"explain":"Results that agree each time give more confidence in the conclusion. Science still stays open to new evidence.","level":2},
      {"q":"A farmer uses knowledge about how the Sun's heat can cook food to make a solar cooker. The solar cooker is an example of","options":["a hypothesis","a variable","evidence","technology"],"answer":3,"explain":"A solar cooker is a useful object made by using scientific knowledge, so it is technology.","level":2},
      {"q":"Assertion (A): A hypothesis that is not supported by the results is still useful to a scientist.\nReason (R): It tells the scientist to change the idea and test again.","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],"answer":0,"explain":"When the results do not support a hypothesis, scientists change it and test again. This is the reason the failed hypothesis is still useful.","level":3,"keepOrder":true},
      {"q":"Ravi gives a fertiliser to one tomato plant kept in the sun and does not give it to another plant kept in the shade. Both plants are the same size. The first plant grows taller. Why can he NOT conclude that the fertiliser caused this?","options":["Tomato plants do not respond to fertilisers","Two things, fertiliser and sunlight, were changed together","A taller plant is always the healthier plant","Plants grow taller only in the shade"],"answer":1,"explain":"A fair test changes only one variable. Here fertiliser and sunlight both differ, so the difference in growth could be due to either.","level":3},
      {"q":"Sunita sets up seeds on cotton and counts how many of 10 seeds sprouted after three days.\nDry cotton, in light: 0 sprouted\nDamp cotton, in light: 9 sprouted\nDamp cotton, in a dark cupboard: 9 sprouted\nWhich conclusion is best supported by these results?","options":["Light is necessary for seeds to sprout, because 9 seeds sprouted in light","Water makes no difference to sprouting, because seeds sprouted in the dark","Damp cotton made a difference, but light made no difference to sprouting in this test","Seeds sprout better in the dark than in light in every case"],"answer":2,"explain":"The seeds sprouted on damp cotton in both light and dark, but not on dry cotton. So water mattered here, while light did not.","level":3},
      {"q":"Neha guessed that salt dissolves faster in hotter water. Her results are:\nWater at 10 °C: 90 seconds\nWater at 40 °C: 30 seconds\nWater at 70 °C: 12 seconds\nWhat should she write in her conclusion?","options":["The results do not support her hypothesis, because 70 °C took the shortest time","The results show that salt dissolves faster in colder water than in hot water","The results show that the time taken does not depend on the temperature of the water","The results support her hypothesis: the hotter the water, the less time the salt took to dissolve"],"answer":3,"explain":"Time decreased as temperature rose, which matches her guess. Her conclusion is that the results support the hypothesis, as long as the amount of salt and water was kept the same.","level":3},
      {"q":"A student says, 'Science cannot be trusted because its ideas keep changing.' Which reply is best?","options":["Ideas change when new evidence shows that they were incomplete, so science keeps getting more accurate","Scientists change their ideas whenever they feel like it, so no idea can be trusted","Old scientific ideas were always completely wrong, and the new ones are always right","Ideas cannot change once they are written in textbooks, so they are final"],"answer":0,"explain":"Scientific ideas rest on evidence. Better instruments and experiments can improve or replace an earlier idea, which makes our understanding more accurate.","level":3}
    ],
    written: [
      {"q":"Define science. Name the three main branches mentioned in the chapter and say what each studies.","a":"Science is the systematic study of nature and the world around us through observation, questions and experiments.\nPhysics studies matter, energy and forces.\nChemistry studies substances and their changes.\nBiology studies living things.","marks":3,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"What is a hypothesis? Write one hypothesis that you can test using ice cubes.","a":"A hypothesis is a testable guess or possible explanation that is checked by experiments.\nExample: Ice melts faster in warm water than in cold water.","marks":2,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"Give two reasons why scientists repeat their experiments and keep careful records.","a":"So that other people can check the results.\nWhen the same result comes again and again, the conclusion becomes more reliable.","marks":2,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"Name any two technologies and write the problem each one helps to solve.","a":"Vaccine – prevents a disease.\nSolar cooker – cooks food using the Sun's heat.\n(Also acceptable: mobile phone – talking to people far away; weather satellite – watching clouds and weather.)","marks":2,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"Write the names of two Indian scientists from the chapter and the field in which each worked.","a":"Any two: Aryabhata – astronomy and mathematics; Charaka – medicine; Sushruta – surgery; J. C. Bose – showed that plants respond to stimuli; C. V. Raman – scattering of light (Nobel Prize in Physics, 1930).","marks":2,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"What is meant by a scientific attitude? Write three qualities of a person who has it.","a":"A scientific attitude means thinking and acting like a scientist.\nAny three: curious; honest about results; open to new ideas; asks for evidence instead of believing everything heard.","marks":3,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"The branches of science overlap. Explain this with one example.","a":"Branches of science depend on one another.\nExample: to understand how a plant makes food, we need biology (the living plant), chemistry (the substances formed) and physics (the energy from light) together.","marks":2,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"Case study: Meera wants to know whether seeds need water to sprout. She puts 10 seeds on damp cotton in one plate and 10 seeds on dry cotton in another plate. Both plates are kept on the same shelf at the same temperature. After four days, 9 seeds on the damp cotton have sprouted and none on the dry cotton.\n(i) What is the variable in this experiment?\n(ii) Write two things Meera kept the same.\n(iii) What conclusion can she draw?","a":"(i) The water (damp cotton or dry cotton).\n(ii) Any two: the number of seeds, the type of seeds, the place on the shelf, the temperature, the time.\n(iii) Seeds need water to sprout. Repeating the experiment would make the conclusion more reliable.","marks":4,"type":"case"},
      {"q":"A classmate says, 'Science is only a list of facts that never changes.' Do you agree? Give three points.","a":"No, I do not agree.\nScientific ideas are based on evidence, so a new piece of evidence can improve or replace an old idea.\nExample: people once believed the Sun and planets go around the Earth; careful observations showed that Earth and the other planets go around the Sun.\nBetter instruments such as microscopes, telescopes and satellites help scientists find new evidence, so science keeps growing.","marks":4,"type":"hots"},
      {"q":"Can there be technology without science? Explain your answer with two examples.","a":"Technology is the use of scientific knowledge to make useful things, so it depends on science.\nExample 1: knowing that sunlight gives heat (science) led to the solar cooker (technology).\nExample 2: knowing how germs cause disease (science) led to the vaccine (technology).\nWithout the knowledge from science, these useful things could not be designed.","marks":4,"type":"hots"}
    ]
  },
  "exploring-substances-acidic-basic-and-neutral": {
    mcq: [
      {"q":"Tamarind tastes sour because it contains","options":["citric acid","tartaric acid","lactic acid","formic acid"],"answer":1,"explain":"Tamarind contains tartaric acid. Lemon juice has citric acid, curd has lactic acid and an ant's sting has formic acid.","level":1,"obj":"acids-bases-and-neutral-substances"},
      {"q":"Vinegar contains which acid?","options":["Lactic acid","Hydrochloric acid","Acetic acid","Formic acid"],"answer":2,"explain":"Vinegar contains acetic acid. Curd has lactic acid, the stomach makes hydrochloric acid and ants inject formic acid.","level":1,"obj":"acids-bases-and-neutral-substances"},
      {"q":"Which of these does not change the colour of either red or blue litmus paper?","options":["Lemon juice","Soap solution","Lime water","Pure water"],"answer":3,"explain":"Pure water is neutral. Lemon juice is an acid, and soap solution and lime water are bases, so each of them changes one colour of litmus.","level":1,"obj":"acids-bases-and-neutral-substances"},
      {"q":"Litmus, the indicator used in the laboratory, is a dye obtained from","options":["lichens","red cabbage","turmeric","China rose"],"answer":0,"explain":"Litmus is obtained from lichens. Red cabbage, turmeric and China rose are other natural indicators.","level":1,"obj":"indicators"},
      {"q":"A strip of turmeric paper is dipped in lemon juice. What happens to its colour?","options":["It turns reddish-brown","It stays yellow","It turns blue","It turns green"],"answer":1,"explain":"Turmeric changes colour only with a base, when it turns reddish-brown. Lemon juice is an acid, so the paper stays yellow.","level":1,"obj":"indicators"},
      {"q":"Hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide are called corrosive because they","options":["always taste sweet","turn pure water red","can burn the skin","change the colour of litmus only"],"answer":2,"explain":"Strong acids and bases are corrosive, which means they can burn the skin. That is why unknown substances are never touched or tasted.","level":1,"obj":"acids-bases-and-neutral-substances"},
      {"q":"Which acid does the stomach make to digest food?","options":["Acetic acid","Citric acid","Tartaric acid","Hydrochloric acid"],"answer":3,"explain":"The stomach makes hydrochloric acid to help digest food. Too much of it causes acidity.","level":1,"obj":"neutralisation-in-daily-life"},
      {"q":"The active substance in milk of magnesia is","options":["magnesium hydroxide","magnesium chloride","citric acid","sodium chloride"],"answer":0,"explain":"Milk of magnesia contains magnesium hydroxide, a base that neutralises excess acid in the stomach.","level":1,"obj":"neutralisation-in-daily-life"},
      {"q":"Methyl orange is a laboratory indicator. What colour does it show in lemon juice?","options":["Yellow","Red","Pink","Colourless"],"answer":1,"explain":"Methyl orange is red in acids and yellow in bases. Lemon juice is an acid.","level":2,"obj":"indicators"},
      {"q":"A few drops of phenolphthalein are added to vinegar. The colour of the mixture is","options":["pink","yellow","colourless","reddish-brown"],"answer":2,"explain":"Phenolphthalein is colourless in acids and pink in bases. Vinegar is an acid, so there is no colour.","level":2,"obj":"indicators"},
      {"q":"Ria adds dilute hydrochloric acid to sodium hydroxide solution in a test tube and holds the tube in her hand. What does she feel?","options":["The tube becomes very cold","Nothing changes","The tube shakes because of a loud sound","The tube becomes warm"],"answer":3,"explain":"Heat is given out during neutralisation, so the mixture becomes warm.","level":2,"obj":"neutralisation"},
      {"q":"Which statement about neutralisation is correct?","options":["Acid + base → salt + water","Acid + base → salt + hydrogen","Acid + base → salt + carbon dioxide","Acid + base → only water"],"answer":0,"explain":"The products of neutralisation are always a salt and water. A gas is not a product of this reaction.","level":2,"obj":"neutralisation"},
      {"q":"After a heavy festival meal, Gurdeep feels a burning pain in the stomach due to acidity. Which of these should he take?","options":["A glass of fresh lemon juice with a little sugar","An antacid such as milk of magnesia","A spoon of vinegar mixed with water","A cold drink made from sour tamarind pulp"],"answer":1,"explain":"Acidity means too much acid in the stomach. An antacid is a mild base and neutralises it, while lemon juice, vinegar and tamarind would add more acid.","level":2,"obj":"neutralisation-in-daily-life"},
      {"q":"Besides baking soda, which of these can be applied to soothe an ant sting?","options":["Lemon juice","Vinegar","Calamine solution","Tamarind pulp"],"answer":2,"explain":"The sting contains formic acid. Calamine solution is a mild base and neutralises it, while the other three are acids.","level":2,"obj":"neutralisation-in-daily-life"},
      {"q":"The soil in Harpreet's vegetable patch turns red litmus blue when tested. What should she mix into the soil to improve it?","options":["Slaked lime","Quicklime","Baking soda","Compost"],"answer":3,"explain":"Compost (organic matter) is added to basic soil. Lime is a base, so it is used for acidic soil, not for basic soil.","level":2,"obj":"neutralisation-in-daily-life"},
      {"q":"Aman eats many sweets and goes to bed without brushing his teeth. Why is this harmful for his teeth?","options":["Bacteria turn the leftover sugar into acids that damage the enamel","The sugar turns into a base that burns the teeth","The sweets make toothpaste acidic","Sugar is neutral, so the teeth are cleaned by it"],"answer":0,"explain":"Bacteria in the mouth change leftover sugar into acids. Brushing removes the sugar, and basic toothpaste helps to neutralise the acids.","level":2,"obj":"neutralisation-in-daily-life"},
      {"q":"Three colourless solutions are tested with litmus paper.\nX: blue litmus turns red; red litmus no change\nY: red litmus turns blue; blue litmus no change\nZ: no change in either litmus paper\nWhich solution is neutral?","options":["X","Z","Y","X and Y"],"answer":1,"explain":"A neutral solution changes neither litmus paper. X is acidic and Y is basic.","level":2,"obj":"identify-unknown"},
      {"q":"Ritu adds red cabbage juice to a clear solution and it turns green. The solution is","options":["acidic","neutral","basic","a strong acid"],"answer":2,"explain":"Red cabbage juice turns pinkish-red in acids and green in bases.","level":2,"obj":"indicators"},
      {"q":"Water is evaporated from a mixture formed when hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide neutralise each other. What is left behind?","options":["Hydrogen gas formed in the reaction","Pure hydrochloric acid in solid form","A soapy solid that is a base","Common salt (sodium chloride)"],"answer":3,"explain":"Neutralisation gives a salt and water: hydrochloric acid + sodium hydroxide → sodium chloride + water. When the water evaporates, the salt remains.","level":2,"obj":"neutralisation"},
      {"q":"Raju is stung by an ant. His friend says, 'Rub lemon juice on it.' Why is this NOT a good idea?","options":["Lemon juice is an acid and will add more acid to the sting","Lemon juice is a base and will burn the skin","Lemon juice is neutral and does nothing","Lemon juice removes formic acid by turning it into water"],"answer":0,"explain":"An ant sting contains formic acid and needs a mild base. Lemon juice is also an acid, so it would not neutralise the sting.","level":2,"obj":"neutralisation-in-daily-life"},
      {"q":"Anita rubs soap on a turmeric stain and the stain turns reddish-brown. Then she squeezes lemon juice on the red patch. What will she most probably see?","options":["The patch becomes darker red","The patch gradually becomes yellow again","The patch turns blue","The patch turns colourless"],"answer":1,"explain":"Soap is a base, so turmeric turns reddish-brown. Lemon juice, an acid, neutralises the soap, and turmeric is yellow once no base is left.","level":3,"obj":"neutralisation"},
      {"q":"Assertion (A): Taking an antacid tablet relieves acidity.\nReason (R): The tablet contains a base that neutralises the extra acid in the stomach.","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],"answer":0,"explain":"Acidity is caused by too much acid in the stomach. The base in an antacid neutralises it, so R correctly explains A.","level":3,"obj":"neutralisation-in-daily-life","keepOrder":true},
      {"q":"A farmer tests a sample of his field soil mixed with water. Blue litmus turns red. He adds slaked lime to the field. Later the same test shows no change in red or blue litmus. What does this show?","options":["The soil has become more acidic","The soil has become basic","The soil has become an indicator","The soil has become neutral"],"answer":3,"explain":"Slaked lime is a base. It cancelled the acid in the soil, so neither litmus changes any more and the soil is neutral.","level":3,"obj":"neutralisation-in-daily-life"},
      {"q":"Four solutions were tested.\nA: turmeric turns reddish-brown; China rose turns green\nB: blue litmus turns red; turmeric stays yellow\nC: no change with litmus or turmeric\nD: China rose turns dark pink; phenolphthalein stays colourless\nWhich row correctly classifies A, B, C and D?","options":["A base, B acid, C neutral, D acid","A acid, B base, C neutral, D base","A base, B acid, C neutral, D base","A neutral, B acid, C base, D acid"],"answer":0,"explain":"Reddish-brown turmeric and green China rose mean a base. Blue litmus turning red with turmeric staying yellow (B), and dark pink China rose with colourless phenolphthalein (D), both mean an acid. No change with all indicators means neutral.","level":3,"obj":"identify-unknown"},
      {"q":"A student tests a colourless solution with phenolphthalein. It stays colourless, so she writes 'The solution is neutral.' Why is her conclusion not safe?","options":["Phenolphthalein turns pink in neutral solutions, so colourless means it is not neutral","Phenolphthalein is colourless in both acids and neutral solutions, so she should also use litmus paper","Phenolphthalein can never be used to test a colourless liquid of any kind","A colourless result with phenolphthalein always means that the solution is a base"],"answer":1,"explain":"Phenolphthalein shows pink only in bases. A colourless result can mean acid or neutral, so another indicator such as red and blue litmus is needed before deciding.","level":3,"obj":"identify-unknown"}
    ],
    written: [
      {"q":"Give two examples of acids and two examples of bases that are found in a kitchen.","a":"Acids: any two of vinegar, lemon juice, curd, tamarind.\nBases: any two of soap, baking soda, lime water (also acceptable: milk of magnesia from the medicine box).","marks":2,"type":"short","obj":"acids-bases-and-neutral-substances"},
      {"q":"Why are strong acids and bases not tasted or touched? Name one strong acid and one strong base.","a":"Strong acids and bases are corrosive and can burn the skin.\nStrong acid: hydrochloric acid. Strong base: sodium hydroxide.","marks":2,"type":"short","obj":"acids-bases-and-neutral-substances"},
      {"q":"State the colour shown by red cabbage juice and by phenolphthalein in an acid and in a base.","a":"Red cabbage juice: pinkish-red in an acid and green in a base.\nPhenolphthalein: colourless in an acid and pink in a base.","marks":2,"type":"short","obj":"indicators"},
      {"q":"What is formed when an acid and a base are mixed? State one change you can notice in the mixture.","a":"A salt and water are formed (neutralisation).\nHeat is given out, so the mixture becomes warm.","marks":2,"type":"short","obj":"neutralisation"},
      {"q":"Why do farmers add lime to some soils? What do they add to basic soil?","a":"Lime is a base. It neutralises the extra acid in acidic soil.\nCompost (organic matter) is added to basic soil.","marks":2,"type":"short","obj":"neutralisation-in-daily-life"},
      {"q":"Why is it a good habit to brush your teeth after eating sweets?","a":"Bacteria in the mouth turn leftover sugar into acids that damage the enamel.\nBrushing removes the sugar, and toothpaste, which is basic, helps to neutralise the acids.","marks":2,"type":"short","obj":"neutralisation-in-daily-life"},
      {"q":"A solution stays colourless when phenolphthalein is added. Can we say that it is acidic? Explain.","a":"No. Phenolphthalein is colourless in an acid and also in a neutral solution.\nWe should also test with red and blue litmus before deciding.","marks":2,"type":"short","obj":"identify-unknown"},
      {"q":"Case study: Gurpreet's wheat crop is growing poorly. He mixes a sample of soil with water and dips blue litmus paper in it. The paper turns red.\n(i) What does this test show about the soil?\n(ii) Name one substance he can add to the field to improve it and say why.\n(iii) Why is it better to use litmus paper than to taste the soil?","a":"(i) The soil is acidic.\n(ii) Slaked lime or quicklime (a base), because it neutralises the extra acid.\n(iii) Unknown substances must never be tasted. Litmus gives a safe result.","marks":4,"type":"case","obj":"neutralisation-in-daily-life"},
      {"q":"Describe an activity to show neutralisation using phenolphthalein, dilute hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide solution.","a":"Take a little sodium hydroxide solution in a test tube and add 2 to 3 drops of phenolphthalein. The solution turns pink because it is a base.\nAdd dilute hydrochloric acid drop by drop, shaking after each drop.\nThe pink colour fades and finally disappears when the base has been neutralised.\nThe test tube feels warm because heat is given out.\nThe products are sodium chloride (a salt) and water: HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O.","marks":5,"type":"long","obj":"neutralisation"},
      {"q":"A white shirt has a curry stain. When it was washed with soap the stain turned red. When the same shirt was soaked in lemon water the red colour faded to yellow. Explain both observations.","a":"Curry contains turmeric, a natural indicator.\nSoap is a base, and turmeric turns reddish-brown with a base, so the stain turned red.\nLemon juice is an acid. It neutralises the soap, and turmeric is yellow again when no base is left.","marks":4,"type":"hots","obj":"indicators"}
    ]
  },
  "electricity-circuits-and-their-components": {
    mcq: [
      {"q":"An electric cell changes","options":["electrical energy into chemical energy","light energy into heat energy","chemical energy into electrical energy","heat energy into sound energy"],"answer":2,"explain":"A cell stores chemical energy and changes it into electrical energy when it is connected in a circuit.","level":1,"obj":"cell-battery-and-circuit"},
      {"q":"By convention, in a circuit with a cell and a bulb, current is taken to flow from","options":["the negative terminal through the bulb to the positive terminal","the bulb to the switch only","both terminals towards the bulb at the same time","the positive terminal through the bulb to the negative terminal"],"answer":3,"explain":"By convention, current flows out of the + terminal, through the wires and bulb, and back to the − terminal.","level":1,"obj":"cell-battery-and-circuit"},
      {"q":"The part of an ordinary bulb that gets hot and gives light is the","options":["filament","glass bulb","metal cap","switch"],"answer":0,"explain":"The thin wire called the filament is heated by the current until it glows.","level":1,"obj":"bulb-and-led"},
      {"q":"In a circuit diagram, a switch is drawn with a gap between its ends. This means the switch is","options":["closed (ON)","open (OFF)","fused","positive"],"answer":1,"explain":"A switch drawn with a gap is open, so the circuit is broken. Drawn joined, it is closed (ON).","level":1,"obj":"circuit-symbols-and-diagrams"},
      {"q":"The outer covering of an electric cable is made of plastic or rubber. This is done because these materials","options":["are good conductors of electricity","make the current flow faster","are insulators and protect us from shocks","make the wires cheaper to buy"],"answer":2,"explain":"The wire inside is a conductor such as copper or aluminium. The plastic or rubber covering is an insulator that keeps us safe.","level":1,"obj":"conductors-and-insulators"},
      {"q":"Home wiring is done in","options":["series","a single loop with no switch","a circle with all appliances on one path","parallel"],"answer":3,"explain":"In parallel wiring each appliance has its own path, so one appliance can be switched off or fail without stopping the others.","level":1,"obj":"series-parallel-and-the-fuse"},
      {"q":"Which of these appliances works because of the heating effect of current?","options":["Electric iron","Wall switch","Torch cell","Plastic socket cover"],"answer":0,"explain":"An electric iron has a part that becomes hot when current flows. The other three do not produce heat for use.","level":1,"obj":"heating-effect-and-fuse"},
      {"q":"The black lead inside a pencil, graphite, is a","options":["insulator of electricity","conductor of electricity","fuse wire","source of electricity"],"answer":1,"explain":"Graphite conducts electricity even though it is not a metal.","level":1,"obj":"conductors-and-insulators"},
      {"q":"A toy car needs a battery of 6 V. How many 1.5 V cells must be joined in series for it?","options":["2","3","4","6"],"answer":2,"explain":"In series, the voltages add up. 4 × 1.5 V = 6 V.","level":2,"obj":"cell-battery-and-circuit"},
      {"q":"Two cells are placed in a torch with the + end of one touching the + end of the other. The torch does not work properly. What is the most likely reason?","options":["The cells are too large for the torch","Cells cannot be used in torches","The cells are too cold","The cells are not joined + to −"],"answer":3,"explain":"In a battery, the + terminal of one cell must touch the − terminal of the next. Two + ends touching is the wrong way to connect them.","level":2,"obj":"cell-battery-and-circuit"},
      {"q":"Rohit connects an LED to a cell and it does not glow. The wires are tight and the cell is fresh. What should he try first?","options":["Reverse the LED so that its longer lead goes to the + side","Replace the LED with one that has longer leads","Keep the cell in the freezer for some time first","Cover the LED with a layer of plastic tape"],"answer":0,"explain":"An LED glows only when its longer lead is connected towards the + side. Turning it round is the first thing to try.","level":2,"obj":"bulb-and-led"},
      {"q":"How can you tell that a filament bulb is fused by looking at it?","options":["The bulb feels hot even when it is switched off","The filament inside is broken","The glass bulb is round","The metal cap is shiny"],"answer":1,"explain":"A fused bulb has a broken filament, so the circuit is not complete and no current flows.","level":2,"obj":"bulb-and-led"},
      {"q":"Bulbs P and Q are joined in parallel to a cell. Q has its own switch and P has none. The switch of Q is turned OFF. What happens?","options":["Both bulbs go off","Q glows and P goes off","P keeps glowing and Q goes off","Both bulbs glow brighter"],"answer":2,"explain":"Each bulb in a parallel circuit has its own path. Opening Q's path does not break P's path.","level":2,"obj":"series-parallel-and-the-fuse"},
      {"q":"Rohan tests some objects by placing each in a gap in a circuit with a bulb. Which set of objects will make the bulb glow?","options":["A rubber eraser and a plastic scale","A dry wooden stick and a glass marble","A rubber band and a plastic comb","An iron key and a steel spoon"],"answer":3,"explain":"Iron and steel are metals, so they are conductors and complete the circuit. The others are insulators.","level":2,"obj":"conductors-and-insulators"},
      {"q":"Two wires from a cell and a bulb are dipped in a glass of water containing salt. The bulb glows. What does this show?","options":["Salt water conducts electricity","Salt water is an insulator","Only wires can conduct electricity","The cell made the salt melt"],"answer":0,"explain":"The water completes the circuit, so salt water is a conductor.","level":2,"obj":"conductors-and-insulators"},
      {"q":"A fuse in a house melts again and again. An electrician puts a thick copper wire in its place. Why is this dangerous?","options":["The thick wire is an insulator, so no current can pass through the circuit","The thick wire will not melt, so the circuit is no longer protected from too much current","Copper is not able to carry any electricity at all in a house","The thick wire will store the extra current and release it slowly"],"answer":1,"explain":"A fuse must have a thin wire that melts when the current is too large. A thick copper wire does not melt, so appliances and wiring could be damaged or catch fire.","level":2,"obj":"heating-effect-and-fuse"},
      {"q":"In a simple circuit with a cell and one bulb, a student says, 'The bulb uses up the current, so less current comes back to the cell.' What is wrong with this?","options":["Nothing is wrong, because the current is used up as it passes through the bulb","The current goes only from the − terminal to the bulb and stops there","The current is the same all the way round the circuit; the bulb changes electrical energy into light and heat","The connecting wires use up the current as it travels along them"],"answer":2,"explain":"Current does not get used up. It is the same throughout a simple circuit. The bulb transforms the electrical energy into light and heat.","level":2,"obj":"cell-battery-and-circuit"},
      {"q":"In a circuit diagram the switch symbol is drawn with its ends joined, and the circuit is otherwise complete with a cell and a bulb. What happens to the bulb?","options":["It stays off","It is fused","It glows only if the cell is turned round","It glows"],"answer":3,"explain":"A switch drawn joined is closed (ON), so the circuit is complete and the bulb glows.","level":2,"obj":"circuit-symbols-and-diagrams"},
      {"q":"An electrician uses a screwdriver with a plastic handle. What is the reason for this?","options":["Plastic is an insulator and protects the electrician from electric shock","Plastic is a good conductor, so it lets the current pass quickly","Plastic makes the metal of the screwdriver stronger","Plastic helps the current to reach the screw faster"],"answer":0,"explain":"Plastic does not allow current to pass through, so it protects the hand.","level":2,"obj":"conductors-and-insulators"},
      {"q":"Which statement correctly compares a fuse with an MCB?","options":["A fuse switches on again by itself, but an MCB has to be replaced every time","An MCB switches off automatically and can be switched on again, but a melted fuse wire has to be replaced","Both of them can be used only once and then have to be thrown away","A fuse protects against too much heat, and an MCB protects against too much cold"],"answer":1,"explain":"Both break the circuit when the current is too high. An MCB can be turned on again, but a fuse wire that has melted must be replaced.","level":2,"obj":"heating-effect-and-fuse"},
      {"q":"Assertion (A): The wire used in a fuse is thin.\nReason (R): A thin wire melts easily when too much current flows through it.","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],"answer":0,"explain":"The fuse wire must melt quickly to break the circuit when the current is too high. A thin wire melts easily, so R explains A.","level":3,"obj":"heating-effect-and-fuse","keepOrder":true},
      {"q":"Some materials are tested in a circuit with a bulb:\nCopper wire: bulb glows\nPencil lead: bulb glows\nPlastic scale: bulb does not glow\nDry wood: bulb does not glow\nWhich conclusion is correct?","options":["Only metals can conduct electricity","Plastic is a better conductor than copper","Dry wood and pencil lead are both insulators","Pencil lead is a conductor even though it is not a metal"],"answer":3,"explain":"Pencil lead is graphite, which conducts electricity although it is not a metal. Plastic and dry wood are insulators.","level":3,"obj":"conductors-and-insulators"},
      {"q":"Four 1.5 V cells are joined in series to make a 6 V battery that is connected to a bulb. One cell is taken out of the chain and the gap is left empty. What happens to the bulb?","options":["It does not glow, because the circuit is broken","It glows with a 4.5 V battery","It glows brighter","It glows, but only for a short time"],"answer":0,"explain":"Cells in series form part of one path. Taking one out leaves a gap, so the circuit is open and no current flows.","level":3,"obj":"cell-battery-and-circuit"},
      {"q":"In a kitchen, the MCB switches off when a heater, an iron and a toaster are all plugged into one extension board. What is the best next step?","options":["Replace the MCB with a thick copper wire","Switch off some of the appliances and then switch the MCB on again","Keep pressing the MCB on while all the appliances stay connected","Wrap the extension board in plastic and leave it connected"],"answer":1,"explain":"Too many appliances draw too much current, and the heating effect makes the MCB cut off the supply. Once the load is reduced, an MCB can be switched on again. A thick copper wire would remove the protection.","level":3,"obj":"heating-effect-and-fuse"},
      {"q":"A cell, a bulb and an LED are connected in series. The LED is connected the wrong way round. What do you observe?","options":["Only the bulb glows, because the bulb works either way round","Only the LED glows, because it is connected last","Neither the LED nor the bulb glows","Both glow, but only dimly"],"answer":2,"explain":"The LED works in only one direction. Connected the wrong way, it breaks the single path of the series circuit, so the bulb also stays off.","level":3,"obj":"bulb-and-led"}
    ],
    written: [
      {"q":"What is the difference between an electric cell and a battery?","a":"A cell is a single unit with a positive and a negative terminal.\nA battery is two or more cells joined together, the + terminal of one touching the − terminal of the next, so their voltages add up.","marks":2,"type":"short","obj":"cell-battery-and-circuit"},
      {"q":"A toy needs a 6 V battery and you have 1.5 V cells. How many cells do you need and how will you join them?","a":"Number of cells = 6 V ÷ 1.5 V = 4.\nJoin them in series, with the + terminal of one cell touching the − terminal of the next.","marks":2,"type":"short","obj":"cell-battery-and-circuit"},
      {"q":"How can you identify the positive terminal in (a) a circuit symbol of a cell and (b) a real LED?","a":"(a) In the symbol of a cell, the longer thin line is positive (+).\n(b) In an LED, the longer lead is positive.","marks":2,"type":"short","obj":"circuit-symbols-and-diagrams"},
      {"q":"What is a fused bulb? Why does it not glow?","a":"A fused bulb is a bulb whose filament has broken.\nThe broken filament breaks the circuit, so no current can flow and the bulb does not glow.","marks":2,"type":"short","obj":"bulb-and-led"},
      {"q":"A pencil lead and a rubber eraser are placed one by one in a gap in a circuit with a bulb. What will you observe in each case? Give reasons.","a":"With the pencil lead the bulb glows, because graphite is a conductor and completes the circuit.\nWith the rubber eraser the bulb does not glow, because rubber is an insulator.","marks":2,"type":"short","obj":"conductors-and-insulators"},
      {"q":"Two bulbs are in series and two other bulbs are in parallel. One bulb fuses in each pair. Describe what happens to the bulb that is not fused in each pair.","a":"Series pair: the other bulb also goes off, because there is only one path and it is broken.\nParallel pair: the other bulb keeps glowing, because it has its own path.","marks":3,"type":"short","obj":"series-parallel-and-the-fuse"},
      {"q":"What happens to the wire of a fuse when too much current flows? Why is a fuse wire made thin?","a":"The fuse wire gets hot and melts, which breaks the circuit and protects the appliances.\nIt is thin so that it melts easily when the current becomes too large.","marks":2,"type":"short","obj":"heating-effect-and-fuse"},
      {"q":"Case study: Aarav tests some objects by placing each in a gap in a circuit that has a cell, a bulb and wires.\nIron nail: bulb glows\nPlastic ruler: bulb does not glow\nSalt water in a cup: bulb glows\nRubber band: bulb does not glow\n(i) Which objects are conductors?\n(ii) Why does the bulb not glow with the plastic ruler?\n(iii) Why should we never touch a switch with wet hands?","a":"(i) The iron nail and the salt water.\n(ii) Plastic is an insulator, so it does not let current pass, the circuit stays open and the bulb does not glow.\n(iii) Ordinary water conducts electricity, so wet hands could let current pass through the body and cause an electric shock.","marks":4,"type":"case","obj":"conductors-and-insulators"},
      {"q":"In a small hall, all the lamps are wrongly connected in series to a single switch. Write three problems that would arise and say how the wiring should be changed.","a":"If one lamp fuses or is removed, all the lamps go off, because there is only one path.\nThe lamps cannot be switched on or off separately.\nIt is hard to find which lamp is fused.\nThe lamps should be connected in parallel (as in home wiring), so each has its own path and the others keep glowing.","marks":4,"type":"hots","obj":"series-parallel-and-the-fuse"},
      {"q":"Case study: Mrs Kaur plugs an iron, a toaster and a mixer into one socket at the same time. The lights flicker and the fuse wire melts.\n(i) Why did the fuse wire melt?\n(ii) Why should she not replace it with a thick copper wire?\n(iii) Name a device that can be switched on again after it cuts off the supply.","a":"(i) Too much current flowed through the circuit. The heating effect of current made the thin fuse wire melt and break the circuit.\n(ii) A thick copper wire would not melt, so the circuit would no longer be protected and the wiring could overheat.\n(iii) An MCB, which switches off automatically and can be switched on again.","marks":4,"type":"case","obj":"heating-effect-and-fuse"}
    ]
  },
  "the-world-of-metals-and-non-metals": {
    mcq: [
      {"q":"Steel is made by mixing iron with a little","options":["zinc","sulphur","copper","carbon"],"answer":3,"explain":"Steel is an alloy of iron with a little carbon. Brass is the alloy of copper and zinc.","level":1},
      {"q":"Which non-metal is used to disinfect drinking water?","options":["Chlorine","Nitrogen","Sulphur","Carbon"],"answer":0,"explain":"Chlorine is used to kill germs in drinking water. Nitrogen is used in fertilisers, sulphur in matches and carbon as fuel.","level":1},
      {"q":"The shine on the surface of a freshly cut metal is called","options":["ductility","lustre","brittleness","sonority"],"answer":1,"explain":"Lustre is the shine of a metal. Ductility is being drawn into wires and sonority is making a ringing sound.","level":1},
      {"q":"Which form of carbon is the hardest natural substance?","options":["Graphite","Coal","Diamond","Charcoal"],"answer":2,"explain":"Diamond is the hardest natural substance. Graphite is soft and slippery enough to write with.","level":1},
      {"q":"Which of these non-metals is a gas at room temperature?","options":["Sulphur","Iodine","Phosphorus","Oxygen"],"answer":3,"explain":"Oxygen, nitrogen and hydrogen are gaseous non-metals. Sulphur, iodine and phosphorus are solids.","level":1},
      {"q":"Farmers use fertilisers that contain which non-metal element?","options":["Nitrogen","Mercury","Sodium","Aluminium"],"answer":0,"explain":"Nitrogen is a non-metal that is used in fertilisers.","level":1},
      {"q":"When a piece of magnesium ribbon burns in air, the powder left behind is","options":["black carbon","white magnesium oxide","yellow sulphur","green copper oxide"],"answer":1,"explain":"Magnesium burns with a dazzling white flame and forms white magnesium oxide.","level":1},
      {"q":"A lump of coal is hit hard with a hammer. What is most likely to happen?","options":["It is beaten into a thin sheet","It is drawn into a wire","It breaks into small pieces","It rings like a bell"],"answer":2,"explain":"Carbon in the form of coal is a non-metal, and non-metals are brittle, which means they break into pieces when hammered.","level":1},
      {"q":"Why are electric wires made of copper or aluminium?","options":["These metals are brittle and break easily when they are bent","These metals are poor conductors of electricity, but they are cheap","These metals are liquids at room temperature and flow easily","These metals are good conductors of electricity and can be drawn into wires"],"answer":3,"explain":"Copper and aluminium conduct electricity well and are ductile, so they can be drawn into wires.","level":2},
      {"q":"A gas is formed when zinc reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid. A burning matchstick is brought near the gas. What happens?","options":["A pop sound is heard","The matchstick burns with a brighter flame and no sound","The matchstick goes out silently","A blue smoke rises from the matchstick"],"answer":0,"explain":"The gas is hydrogen. Hydrogen burns with a 'pop' sound.","level":2},
      {"q":"Why does a gold ring stay shiny for hundreds of years?","options":["Gold is a non-metal","Gold is very unreactive","Gold is stored in kerosene","Gold is a liquid"],"answer":1,"explain":"Gold does not easily react with air or water, so it stays shiny. This is why it is used for jewellery.","level":2},
      {"q":"In the reaction Fe + CuSO₄ → FeSO₄ + Cu, which metal is displaced from the solution?","options":["Iron","Sulphur","Copper","Oxygen"],"answer":2,"explain":"Iron is more reactive, so it takes the place of copper in the solution, and copper is pushed out and deposited on the nail.","level":2},
      {"q":"Which pair is correctly matched?","options":["Magnesium oxide – acidic; sulphur dioxide – basic","Magnesium oxide – acidic; sulphur dioxide – acidic","Magnesium oxide – basic; sulphur dioxide – basic","Magnesium oxide – basic; sulphur dioxide – acidic"],"answer":3,"explain":"Oxides of metals are generally basic and oxides of non-metals are generally acidic.","level":2},
      {"q":"Why is stainless steel preferred for kitchen sinks and utensils?","options":["It is an alloy that does not rust easily","It is a non-metal that conducts heat badly","It is a liquid metal","It dissolves in water"],"answer":0,"explain":"Stainless steel is iron mixed with chromium, nickel and carbon, and it resists rusting.","level":2},
      {"q":"Three samples were examined.\nX: dull, breaks when hammered, does not conduct electricity\nY: shiny, drawn into wires, conducts electricity\nZ: shiny, soft enough to be cut with a knife, stored in kerosene\nZ is most likely","options":["sulphur","sodium","iron","copper"],"answer":1,"explain":"Sodium and potassium are soft metals that react very vigorously, so they are stored in kerosene. X looks like a non-metal and Y like a typical metal such as copper.","level":2},
      {"q":"Matches and gunpowder both contain which element?","options":["Chlorine","Iodine","Sulphur","Bromine"],"answer":2,"explain":"Sulphur is used in matches and gunpowder. Chlorine is used to disinfect water and iodine as an antiseptic.","level":2},
      {"q":"Sodium, iron and gold are listed. Which order shows them from the most reactive to the least reactive?","options":["Gold, iron, sodium","Iron, sodium, gold","Sodium, gold, iron","Sodium, iron, gold"],"answer":3,"explain":"Sodium reacts so vigorously that it is stored in kerosene, iron can displace copper from its salt solution, and gold stays shiny for centuries.","level":2},
      {"q":"Which statement about non-metals is an exception to the general rule?","options":["Diamond, a non-metal, is the hardest natural substance","Sulphur, a non-metal, is brittle and breaks easily","Oxygen, a non-metal, is a gas at room temperature","Phosphorus, a non-metal, does not conduct electricity"],"answer":0,"explain":"Most non-metals are not hard, but diamond, a form of carbon, is the hardest natural substance.","level":2},
      {"q":"A copper wire is hammered. Which of these is most likely to happen?","options":["It breaks into small pieces","It flattens and does not break","It melts at once","It turns into a gas"],"answer":1,"explain":"Copper is malleable, so it can be beaten into a flat shape without breaking.","level":2},
      {"q":"In the reaction Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂, which product is the salt?","options":["Hydrogen","Hydrochloric acid","Zinc chloride","Zinc"],"answer":2,"explain":"A metal reacting with a dilute acid gives a salt and hydrogen. Here the salt is zinc chloride and the gas is hydrogen.","level":2},
      {"q":"Assertion (A): All non-metals are solids at room temperature.\nReason (R): Some non-metals, such as oxygen and nitrogen, are gases.","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],"answer":3,"explain":"Non-metals can be gases (oxygen, nitrogen, hydrogen), a liquid (bromine) or solids. So A is false and R is true.","level":3,"keepOrder":true},
      {"q":"Observation 1: A clean iron nail dipped in copper sulphate solution gets a reddish-brown coating.\nObservation 2: A clean copper strip dipped in iron sulphate solution shows no change.\nWhat do the two observations show together?","options":["Iron is more reactive than copper","Copper is more reactive than iron","Both metals are equally reactive","Iron sulphate is a better conductor than copper sulphate"],"answer":0,"explain":"A more reactive metal displaces a less reactive one from its solution. Iron displaced copper but copper could not displace iron.","level":3},
      {"q":"Element Q is shiny and malleable. It burns in air with a dazzling white flame to give a white powder. When the powder is shaken with water and tested, red litmus turns blue. Q is most likely","options":["sulphur","magnesium","carbon","iodine"],"answer":1,"explain":"Magnesium is a metal that burns with a dazzling white flame, and its oxide is basic. Sulphur and carbon are non-metals whose oxides are acidic.","level":3},
      {"q":"Which equation correctly shows the burning of magnesium in air?","options":["Mg + O₂ → MgO","2Mg + 2O₂ → 2MgO","2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO","Mg + 2O₂ → MgO₂"],"answer":2,"explain":"Oxygen is present as O₂ molecules. Two magnesium atoms combine with one oxygen molecule to give two units of magnesium oxide, and only this equation has equal numbers of each kind of atom on both sides.","level":3},
      {"q":"Ritesh says, 'Mercury is a liquid and sodium is soft, so they are not metals at all.' Which reply is correct?","options":["Mercury is a non-metal, but sodium is a metal because it is shiny","Only hard solids with a high melting point can be called metals","Only liquids and soft substances can be called metals","They are metals; most metals are hard solids, but mercury and sodium are exceptions"],"answer":3,"explain":"Most metals are hard and have high melting points, but sodium and potassium are soft and mercury is a liquid at room temperature. They are still metals.","level":3}
    ],
    written: [
      {"q":"Name the property of metals that makes them suitable for (a) a school bell, (b) a cooking vessel and (c) aluminium foil.","a":"(a) Sonorous: metals make a ringing sound when struck.\n(b) Good conductors of heat, so food cooks quickly.\n(c) Malleable: aluminium can be beaten into thin sheets.","marks":3,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"Name three non-metals and write one use of each.","a":"Any three: oxygen – for breathing; nitrogen – in fertilisers; carbon – as fuel (coal, charcoal) and in graphite pencils; sulphur – in matches and gunpowder; chlorine – to disinfect drinking water; iodine – as an antiseptic.","marks":3,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"Write two exceptions in the properties of non-metals.","a":"Graphite, a form of carbon, conducts electricity although it is a non-metal.\nDiamond, another form of carbon, is the hardest natural substance.\n(Also acceptable: iodine is shiny.)","marks":2,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"What do you see when a piece of magnesium ribbon burns in air? Name the product and say whether it is acidic or basic.","a":"It burns with a dazzling white flame and leaves a white powder.\nThe product is magnesium oxide, which is basic (its solution turns red litmus blue).","marks":2,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"Compare the reactivity of sodium with that of gold. Give one line to show each.","a":"Sodium is very reactive; it reacts so vigorously that it is stored in kerosene.\nGold is very unreactive; it stays shiny for centuries, so it is used for jewellery.","marks":2,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"Write the word equation for the reaction of a metal with a dilute acid. How would you test the gas formed?","a":"Metal + dilute acid → salt + hydrogen gas.\nBring a burning matchstick near the gas. Hydrogen burns with a 'pop' sound.","marks":3,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"What is a displacement reaction? Give one example with an equation.","a":"In a displacement reaction a more reactive metal pushes out a less reactive metal from its salt solution.\nExample: Fe + CuSO₄ → FeSO₄ + Cu (iron displaces copper from copper sulphate).","marks":2,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"Case study: Ritu's kitchen has an aluminium pan with a plastic handle, a thin sheet of aluminium foil to pack chapatis, a silver ring and an iron tawa.\n(i) Why is the pan's handle made of plastic?\n(ii) Which property of aluminium allows it to be made into a thin foil?\n(iii) Why do gold and silver ornaments stay shiny for a long time?","a":"(i) Metal is a good conductor of heat, but plastic is a poor conductor, so the handle stays cool and is safe to hold.\n(ii) Malleability: aluminium can be beaten into thin sheets.\n(iii) Gold is very unreactive and does not easily react with air or water, so it keeps its shine. (Silver is also quite unreactive.)","marks":4,"type":"case"},
      {"q":"A student says, 'All metals are hard solids and all non-metals are brittle or dull.' Do you agree? Give examples to support your answer.","a":"No, there are exceptions.\nMetals: sodium and potassium are so soft that they can be cut with a knife, and mercury is a liquid at room temperature.\nNon-metals: graphite conducts electricity, diamond is the hardest natural substance, and iodine is shiny.\nSo the general properties are mostly true but not for every substance.","marks":4,"type":"hots"},
      {"q":"Write the uses of two metals and three non-metals, and state the property for which each metal is used.","a":"Copper – electric wires (good conductor of electricity, ductile).\nAluminium – foil for packing (malleable) or electric wires.\nOxygen – for breathing.\nNitrogen – in fertilisers.\nChlorine – to disinfect drinking water. (Other correct uses from the chapter are also acceptable.)","marks":5,"type":"long"}
    ]
  },
  "changes-around-us-physical-and-chemical": {
    mcq: [
      {"q":"Cutting vegetables into small pieces is a","options":["physical change","chemical change","change in which a new substance is formed","change that always gives out light"],"answer":0,"explain":"Only the size and shape of the vegetable change. No new substance is formed, so it is a physical change.","level":1},
      {"q":"The ripening of a mango on the tree is a","options":["physical change","chemical change","change of state only","change by crystallisation"],"answer":1,"explain":"When a fruit ripens, new substances with a new taste and colour are formed. This is a chemical change.","level":1},
      {"q":"Stretching a rubber band is a","options":["chemical change","change in which gas is formed","physical change","change in which heat and light are given out"],"answer":2,"explain":"A stretched rubber band is still rubber. No new substance is formed, so the change is physical.","level":1},
      {"q":"Burning of LPG in a kitchen stove is a","options":["physical change","reversible change","change of state","chemical change"],"answer":3,"explain":"Burning gives new substances and heat and light. It cannot be reversed, so it is a chemical change.","level":1},
      {"q":"Stainless steel does not rust easily because it is iron mixed with","options":["chromium, nickel and carbon","copper and zinc together","water and a little salt","sulphur and phosphorus"],"answer":0,"explain":"Stainless steel is an alloy of iron with chromium, nickel and carbon. Copper and zinc make brass.","level":1},
      {"q":"A copper vessel left in the open for many months develops a green coating. This is called","options":["crystallisation","corrosion","sublimation","galvanisation"],"answer":1,"explain":"Metals other than iron can also corrode. Copper develops a green coating and silver turns black.","level":1},
      {"q":"Digestion of food in our body is a","options":["physical change","change that forms no new substance","chemical change","reversible change that gives back the food"],"answer":2,"explain":"Food is changed into new substances in the body. Such a change is a chemical change.","level":1},
      {"q":"Salt can be obtained from sea water by","options":["burning","galvanisation","rusting","evaporation"],"answer":3,"explain":"When the water evaporates, the salt is left behind. This is a physical change.","level":1},
      {"q":"Which observation is expected when a magnesium ribbon burns in air?","options":["A dazzling white light and a white powder","A blue flame and a black powder","A red flame with no residue","A pop sound and a colourless gas"],"answer":0,"explain":"Burning magnesium gives a dazzling white light and white magnesium oxide. We should never look directly at the flame.","level":2},
      {"q":"The gas formed by baking soda and vinegar is passed through lime water. What will be seen, and why?","options":["The lime water turns pink because of the baking soda","The lime water turns milky because insoluble calcium carbonate forms","The lime water becomes colourless because vinegar is an acid","The lime water boils because heat is given out"],"answer":1,"explain":"The gas is carbon dioxide. It reacts with the calcium hydroxide in lime water to form insoluble calcium carbonate, which makes it milky.","level":2},
      {"q":"Why does applying grease to an iron axle help to prevent rusting?","options":["It turns the iron into stainless steel","It makes iron react with sunlight","It keeps air and water away from the iron","It changes iron into zinc"],"answer":2,"explain":"Rusting needs both oxygen and water. A layer of grease keeps them from touching the iron.","level":2},
      {"q":"Which of these changes can be reversed easily?","options":["Burning of paper","Souring of milk","Rusting of an iron nail","Melting of ice to water"],"answer":3,"explain":"Melting and freezing of water is a physical change that can be reversed. The other three are chemical changes that cannot be undone.","level":2},
      {"q":"A girl tears a sheet of paper into pieces. She cannot join the pieces back easily. Which statement is correct?","options":["It is a physical change, although it is not easily reversed","It is a chemical change because it cannot be reversed","It is a chemical change because the shape changes","It is neither a physical nor a chemical change"],"answer":0,"explain":"No new substance is formed when paper is torn. Some physical changes cannot be undone easily, but they are still physical.","level":2},
      {"q":"Four changes were noted:\nI. Wax melts near a flame\nII. Milk left in a warm place turns sour and smells different\nIII. Water bubbles as it boils\nIV. A paper burns, giving heat, light and ash\nWhich two are chemical changes?","options":["I and III","II and IV","I and IV","II and III"],"answer":1,"explain":"Souring of milk and burning of paper both form new substances. Melting of wax and boiling of water only change the state.","level":2},
      {"q":"Which action will make an iron gate rust faster?","options":["Painting the whole gate again with fresh paint","Oiling the hinges and moving parts regularly","Leaving scratches in the paint so that rain water can reach the iron","Coating the gate with a layer of zinc"],"answer":2,"explain":"Scratches let air and moisture reach the iron. Paint, oil and zinc keep them away.","level":2},
      {"q":"Which of these natural processes is a chemical change?","options":["Drying of a puddle in the sun","Dissolving of salt in water","Melting of butter in a warm kitchen","Making of food by green leaves (photosynthesis)"],"answer":3,"explain":"In photosynthesis new substances are formed from carbon dioxide and water. The others change only the state or the mixing of substances.","level":2},
      {"q":"A hot, saturated solution of copper sulphate is left to cool slowly. What is formed, and what kind of change is it?","options":["Blue crystals of copper sulphate; a physical change","A black powder of a new substance; a chemical change","A colourless gas of a new substance; a chemical change","Rust on the sides of the container; a physical change"],"answer":0,"explain":"On cooling, copper sulphate comes out of the solution as crystals. It is the same substance, so crystallisation is a physical change.","level":2},
      {"q":"Which of these is NOT formed when baking soda reacts with vinegar?","options":["Sodium acetate","Hydrogen gas","Water","Carbon dioxide"],"answer":1,"explain":"Baking soda + vinegar → sodium acetate + water + carbon dioxide. Hydrogen is not formed.","level":2},
      {"q":"Why are ships regularly painted?","options":["To make the iron in the ship lighter in weight","To turn the iron of the ship into a non-metal","To keep air and water away from the iron so that it does not rust","To make the ship float on the sea"],"answer":2,"explain":"Rust weakens iron objects such as ships. Paint prevents iron from touching oxygen and water.","level":2},
      {"q":"Which pair contains one physical change and one chemical change?","options":["Boiling of water and melting of ice","Burning of paper and rusting of iron","Souring of milk and cooking of food","Melting of wax and burning of wax"],"answer":3,"explain":"Melting of wax is physical but burning wax gives new substances. In the other pairs both changes are of the same kind.","level":2},
      {"q":"Assertion (A): Boiling of water is a physical change.\nReason (R): Steam is a new substance with properties different from those of water.","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],"answer":2,"explain":"Boiling only changes water from liquid to vapour. No new substance is formed, so A is true. Steam is still water, so R is false.","level":3,"keepOrder":true},
      {"q":"Four iron nails are kept as shown for two weeks.\n1. In water covered by a layer of oil (no air)\n2. In dry air\n3. Half in water in an open dish\n4. In salty water in an open dish\nWhich nail will show the most rust?","options":["Nail 1","Nail 4","Nail 2","Nail 3"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rusting needs both air and water, and it is faster where the water is salty. Nail 1 has no air, nail 2 has no water, and nail 3 has both but no salt.","level":3},
      {"q":"Jaspreet dissolves a powder in water. Bubbles appear and the mixture becomes slightly warm. She says it must be a chemical change. What should she do next?","options":["Accept her answer, because bubbles always mean a chemical change","Accept her answer, because heat always means a chemical change","Check whether a new substance has been formed","Repeat the test without checking anything else"],"answer":2,"explain":"Bubbles, heat and colour change can also occur in physical changes (like water boiling). The real test is whether a new substance with different properties has formed.","level":3},
      {"q":"Which row correctly labels these changes as physical (P) or chemical (C)?\n(a) Camphor changing directly into vapour\n(b) Burning of a candle, giving soot and carbon dioxide\n(c) Cutting grass","options":["(a) C, (b) C, (c) P","(a) P, (b) P, (c) C","(a) C, (b) P, (c) C","(a) P, (b) C, (c) P"],"answer":3,"explain":"Camphor turning to vapour (sublimation) and cutting grass form no new substance. Burning of the candle forms new substances, so it is chemical.","level":3},
      {"q":"Both crystallisation and rusting produce a solid. Which statement correctly tells them apart?","options":["Crystals are the same substance in a new form, but rust is a new substance","Rusting is a physical change and crystallisation is a chemical change","Crystallisation needs oxygen, and rusting does not","Both form a new substance with different properties"],"answer":0,"explain":"Crystals of copper sulphate are copper sulphate itself, so crystallisation is physical. Rust is hydrated iron oxide, a new substance, so rusting is chemical.","level":3}
    ],
    written: [
      {"q":"Give two examples of physical changes and two of chemical changes that you see in the kitchen.","a":"Physical: any two of melting of butter or ice, dissolving sugar in water, cutting vegetables.\nChemical: any two of burning of LPG, cooking food, souring of milk, milk turning into curd.","marks":2,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"Write four signs of a chemical change. Why is one sign alone not always enough to decide?","a":"Any four: heat or light, gas bubbles, change of colour, a new smell, a sound, a new solid.\nThese signs can also occur in physical changes (for example bubbles in boiling water), so we must ask whether a new substance has formed.","marks":3,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"What do you observe when magnesium ribbon burns? Why should you not look directly at the flame?","a":"It burns with a dazzling white light and leaves a white powder (magnesium oxide).\nThe light is very bright and can harm the eyes.","marks":2,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"Write the word equation for the reaction between baking soda and vinegar and name the gas formed.","a":"Baking soda + vinegar → sodium acetate + water + carbon dioxide.\nThe gas formed is carbon dioxide.","marks":2,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"What is stainless steel? Why is it useful?","a":"Stainless steel is iron mixed with chromium, nickel and carbon.\nIt does not rust easily, so it is used for utensils and sinks.","marks":2,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"What is galvanisation? Why is it done?","a":"Galvanisation is coating iron with a layer of zinc.\nIt keeps air and water away from the iron, so the iron does not rust.","marks":2,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"What is sublimation? Is it a physical or a chemical change? Give a reason.","a":"Sublimation is the change of a solid directly into vapour, for example camphor on heating.\nIt is a physical change because no new substance is formed.","marks":2,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"Case study: Neelam notices four things at home. (a) An ice cube melts in a glass. (b) Milk kept outside overnight has turned sour. (c) A roti puffs up and turns brown on the tawa. (d) A piece of iron left in the courtyard has turned reddish-brown.\n(i) Which of these are chemical changes?\n(ii) Give a reason why melting of ice is a physical change.\n(iii) Name the reddish-brown substance in (d) and the two things needed to form it.","a":"(i) (b), (c) and (d).\n(ii) Only the state changes from solid to liquid; no new substance is formed.\n(iii) Rust (hydrated iron oxide). It needs oxygen (air) and water (moisture).","marks":4,"type":"case"},
      {"q":"Ravi says, 'Bubbles appear when water boils and also when baking soda is added to vinegar, so both are chemical changes.' Is he right? Explain.","a":"Ravi is partly wrong.\nWhen baking soda is added to vinegar, the bubbles are a new gas, carbon dioxide, so it is a chemical change.\nWhen water boils, the bubbles are water vapour (steam). It is still water, so boiling is a physical change.\nBubbles alone are not enough; we must ask whether a new substance has formed.","marks":4,"type":"hots"},
      {"q":"Describe an experiment, with three test tubes, to show that rusting needs both air and water.","a":"Take three test tubes with clean iron nails.\nTube 1: nail in tap water that has been boiled and covered with a layer of oil, so that no air reaches it.\nTube 2: nail with dry air only, in a stoppered tube so that no moisture reaches it.\nTube 3: nail in water, with the tube open to air.\nKeep the tubes for some days. Only the nail in Tube 3, which has both air and water, will show reddish-brown rust.\nThis shows that rusting needs both oxygen (air) and water.","marks":5,"type":"long"}
    ]
  },
  "adolescence-a-stage-of-growth-and-change": {
    mcq: [
      {"q":"Compared with boys, girls generally begin puberty","options":["five years earlier","one or two years earlier","one or two years later","at exactly the same age as every boy"],"answer":1,"explain":"Girls generally begin puberty one or two years earlier than boys, but everyone grows at their own pace.","level":1},
      {"q":"Which organs begin to produce sperms at puberty?","options":["Ovaries","Thyroid glands","Testes","Adrenal glands"],"answer":2,"explain":"The testes begin to produce sperms at puberty, and the ovaries begin to release eggs.","level":1},
      {"q":"Which organs begin to release eggs (ova) at puberty?","options":["Testes","Pancreas","Pituitary","Ovaries"],"answer":3,"explain":"The ovaries begin to release eggs in girls at puberty.","level":1},
      {"q":"The first menstruation in a girl is called","options":["menarche","puberty","goitre","fertilisation"],"answer":0,"explain":"The first menstruation is called menarche. Puberty is the early phase of adolescence.","level":1},
      {"q":"Which hormone is made by the testes?","options":["Oestrogen","Testosterone","Insulin","Thyroxine"],"answer":1,"explain":"The testes make testosterone and the ovaries make oestrogen.","level":1},
      {"q":"Which of these changes is seen in boys but not in girls at puberty?","options":["Hair under the arms","Rapid increase in height","Facial hair","More active sweat glands"],"answer":2,"explain":"Facial hair appears in boys. The other three changes occur in both boys and girls.","level":1},
      {"q":"Pimples often appear at puberty because","options":["the bones become longer and thicker","the heart becomes smaller in size","the voice box grows larger and sticks out","the oil and sweat glands become more active"],"answer":3,"explain":"More active sweat and oil glands cause body odour and pimples.","level":1},
      {"q":"Too little insulin in the body causes","options":["diabetes","goitre","acidity","tooth decay"],"answer":0,"explain":"Insulin made by the pancreas controls blood sugar. Too little of it causes diabetes.","level":1},
      {"q":"How many pairs of chromosomes are there in a human cell?","options":["46","23","11","2"],"answer":1,"explain":"Human cells have 23 pairs of chromosomes, and the last pair are the sex chromosomes.","level":2},
      {"q":"Every human egg carries which sex chromosome?","options":["Y","XY","X","Either X or Y"],"answer":2,"explain":"Every egg carries an X chromosome. A sperm carries either X or Y.","level":2},
      {"q":"Radha's menstrual cycle repeats regularly. About how often does it repeat?","options":["Every 3 to 5 days","Every 6 months","Every 2 years","Every 28 to 30 days"],"answer":3,"explain":"The cycle repeats roughly every 28 to 30 days, while menstruation itself lasts about 3 to 5 days.","level":2},
      {"q":"When does the menstrual cycle stop for a while?","options":["During pregnancy","During sleep","During exercise","During the cold season"],"answer":0,"explain":"The menstrual cycle stops during pregnancy.","level":2},
      {"q":"A 13-year-old boy notices that his voice sometimes cracks. What is the reason?","options":["His heart is growing","His voice box (larynx) is growing","His stomach is making too much acid","His thyroid has stopped working"],"answer":1,"explain":"At puberty the voice box grows, so the voice becomes deeper and may crack for a while.","level":2},
      {"q":"Which factors decide how tall a person grows?","options":["Only the school the person attends","Only the number of friends","Heredity (genes), diet and health","Only the person's birthday month"],"answer":2,"explain":"Height depends on heredity, diet and health. People grow at different paces, so being taller or shorter than friends is normal.","level":2},
      {"q":"What is the best way to look after a face that has pimples?","options":["Squeeze the pimples as soon as they appear","Avoid washing the face","Share a friend's towel to dry it","Wash it regularly and do not squeeze the pimples"],"answer":3,"explain":"Sweat and oil glands are more active at puberty. Wash regularly and do not squeeze pimples.","level":2},
      {"q":"Which diet is the best for a fast-growing teenager?","options":["A balanced diet with proteins, calcium, iron and vitamins","Mostly fried snacks and cold drinks from the market","Only plain rice at every meal of the day","A diet that has no vegetables, fruit or milk in it"],"answer":0,"explain":"A balanced diet supports the fast growth of the body. Too much junk food should be avoided.","level":2},
      {"q":"Dev's friend offers him a cigarette and says, 'Everyone does it.' What is the best response?","options":["Try one to avoid being laughed at","Say no politely and stay away from tobacco","Take it but not smoke it","Keep it to try later"],"answer":1,"explain":"Tobacco, alcohol and drugs harm the body and mind. We should learn to say no to wrong peer pressure.","level":2},
      {"q":"Just before a race, Meena's heart beats faster and she breathes more quickly. Which hormone is mainly responsible?","options":["Insulin","Thyroxine","Adrenaline","Oestrogen"],"answer":2,"explain":"Adrenaline, made by the adrenal glands, prepares the body for an emergency by increasing the heartbeat and breathing rate.","level":2},
      {"q":"Which pair of body-shape changes at adolescence is correct?","options":["Boys – wider hips; girls – broader shoulders","Boys and girls – no change in shape","Boys – shorter arms; girls – longer neck","Boys – broader shoulders; girls – wider hips"],"answer":3,"explain":"At adolescence boys' shoulders become broader and girls' hips become wider.","level":2},
      {"q":"Siddharth feels shy, wants more freedom and is sometimes in a bad mood. Which statement is correct?","options":["These feelings are normal at this age, and he can talk to a trusted elder","These feelings mean he is ill and must be treated","He should hide the feelings from everyone","These feelings show he is not growing properly"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mood swings, a wish for independence and feeling shy are normal in adolescence. Talking to parents, teachers or elders helps.","level":2},
      {"q":"Assertion (A): A woman who is pregnant has menstruation every month.\nReason (R): The menstrual cycle stops during pregnancy.","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],"answer":3,"explain":"The menstrual cycle stops during pregnancy, so there is no monthly menstruation. A is false and R is true.","level":3,"keepOrder":true},
      {"q":"A couple has three daughters. Which statement gives the correct scientific explanation?","options":["The mother's egg decides that all of her babies will be girls","The food that the mother eats decides the sex of the baby","In each case an X sperm from the father fertilised the egg, and the sperm decides the sex of the baby","Every egg carries either an X or a Y chromosome, so the egg decides"],"answer":2,"explain":"Every egg has an X chromosome. The sex depends on whether an X sperm (girl, XX) or a Y sperm (boy, XY) fertilises it.","level":3},
      {"q":"A girl's period started on 3 March and her cycle is regular and 28 days long. On which date will her next period most likely start?","options":["28 March","5 April","3 April","31 March"],"answer":3,"explain":"3 March + 28 days = 31 March (March has 31 days). The cycle repeats about every 28 to 30 days.","level":3},
      {"q":"Asha's height was noted every year:\nAge 9: 131 cm\nAge 10: 136 cm\nAge 11: 141 cm\nAge 12: 150 cm\nAge 13: 157 cm\nAge 14: 160 cm\nBetween which ages did she grow the fastest?","options":["11 to 12 years","9 to 10 years","10 to 11 years","13 to 14 years"],"answer":0,"explain":"The gains were 5, 5, 9, 7 and 3 cm. The largest gain, 9 cm, was between 11 and 12 years, in line with the rapid growth in height at puberty.","level":3},
      {"q":"Which statement about the testes and ovaries is correct?","options":["Testes release eggs and make oestrogen; ovaries produce sperms and testosterone","Testes produce sperms and testosterone; ovaries release eggs and make oestrogen","Testes and ovaries make only sperms","Testes and ovaries make only insulin"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each organ has two jobs. The testes make sperms and the hormone testosterone, and the ovaries release eggs and make the hormone oestrogen.","level":3}
    ],
    written: [
      {"q":"What is adolescence? At roughly what age does it occur?","a":"Adolescence is the stage of life between childhood and adulthood.\nIt occurs roughly from about 11 to 18 years of age, and may begin a little earlier or later in different people.","marks":2,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"What are secondary sexual characters? Give one example for boys, one for girls and one that appears in both.","a":"Secondary sexual characters are the features that appear at puberty and show the difference between males and females.\nBoys: facial hair. Girls: breast development. Both: hair under the arms and other body parts.","marks":3,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"What is menstruation? How often does it occur and how long does it last?","a":"Menstruation is the monthly shedding of the thickened lining of the uterus and blood through the vagina when an egg is not fertilised.\nIt repeats roughly every 28 to 30 days and lasts about 3 to 5 days. It is a normal, healthy process.","marks":2,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"Why are endocrine glands called ductless glands? How do their hormones reach different parts of the body?","a":"Endocrine glands do not have ducts (tubes) to carry their secretions.\nThey release hormones directly into the blood, which carries them to different parts of the body.","marks":2,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"Why should pimples not be squeezed? Write two ways of keeping the skin healthy during adolescence.","a":"At puberty the oil and sweat glands are more active and cause pimples; squeezing them is not advised.\nAny two: wash the face regularly, bathe daily, wear clean clothes.","marks":2,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"State the function of the pituitary gland and of adrenaline.","a":"The pituitary gland is the 'master gland'; it controls other glands and releases growth hormone.\nAdrenaline, made by the adrenal glands, prepares the body for emergencies by increasing the heartbeat and breathing rate.","marks":2,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"Two friends of the same age differ in height. Write three factors on which height depends and say whether this difference is a cause for worry.","a":"Height depends on heredity (genes), diet and health.\nEveryone grows at their own pace, so being taller or shorter than friends is normal and not a cause for worry.","marks":3,"type":"short"},
      {"q":"Case study: Neha is 12. Her friend Simran has already begun her periods, but Neha has not. Neha's mother explains that the first menstruation is called menarche and that everyone's body changes at its own pace.\n(i) What happens in the body during menstruation?\n(ii) After about how many days does the cycle usually repeat?\n(iii) Is it a cause for worry that Neha has not yet begun her periods while her friend has? Give a reason.","a":"(i) An egg is not fertilised, so the thickened lining of the uterus and blood are shed through the vagina.\n(ii) About every 28 to 30 days.\n(iii) No. Everyone grows at their own pace, so the age at which puberty begins differs from person to person. She can talk to her mother or a teacher if she is worried.","marks":4,"type":"case"},
      {"q":"Write four things a teenager should do to stay healthy, with a reason for each.","a":"Eat a balanced diet with proteins, calcium, iron and vitamins, because the body is growing fast.\nBathe daily and wear clean clothes, because sweat glands are more active.\nExercise regularly and sleep enough, so that the body and mind stay healthy.\nStay away from tobacco, alcohol and drugs, because they harm the body and mind.\nTalk to parents, teachers or elders about worries (any four with reasons).","marks":4,"type":"long"},
      {"q":"Aman is 13. He feels shy about his changing voice, wants more privacy, and sometimes gets irritated. His mother is worried. Explain what is happening to Aman and give three suggestions.","a":"Aman is going through puberty, the early part of adolescence. His voice box is growing, so his voice is deepening and may crack.\nMood swings, shyness and a wish for independence are normal at this age.\nSuggestions: talk to parents, teachers or trusted elders about worries; eat well, exercise and sleep enough; stay away from tobacco, alcohol and drugs.","marks":4,"type":"hots"}
    ]
  }
});
